      "id": 1,
      "text": "West End began as White Hall in 1835 and was renamed in 1868 after London’s fashionable theater district.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://atlantawestend.com/our-history/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Macon and Western Railroad, opened in the late 1840s, linked West End directly to downtown Atlanta.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://atlantawestend.com/our-history/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "West End emerged in the 19th century as one of Atlanta’s first streetcar suburbs, shaping its residential layout.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://atlanta.com/articles/misc/west-end-atlanta",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Hammonds House Museum, a Victorian home on Peeples Street, displays African and African American fine art.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
